        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Sensors</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these sensors, and nothing on this page should be read as if we had.
            The picks come from manufacturer stores and help centres, Zwift's support documentation,
            independent reviews and retailer listings, all checked on 15 October 2026. Where we say
            a sensor pairs with a watch or app, the maker of the sensor, the maker of the watch or
            Zwift says so.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The first test was whether a product is still on sale, and most of the familiar
            names failed it.</strong> We started from six candidates — Stryd, the Garmin Foot Pod,
            the Garmin Running Dynamics Pod, the COROS POD 2, the Wahoo TICKR X and the Milestone
            Pod — and also checked the Zwift RunPod and the Polar Stride Sensor because Zwift still
            lists them. Here is what we found:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Zwift RunPod:</strong> Zwift's own RunPod FAQ says it has been discontinued
            and is no longer available to purchase. It remains on Zwift's compatibility list, so a
            second-hand one will still pair.</li>
            <li><strong>Garmin Foot Pod:</strong> a Garmin staff reply on Garmin's own forum
            confirms it has been discontinued and points runners to the HRM 600 instead, and
            retailers list it as no longer available. Excluded.</li>
            <li><strong>Garmin Running Dynamics Pod:</strong> several retailers list it as
            discontinued and its US product page on garmin.com no longer loads. It also would not
            have qualified: Garmin forum replies and independent comparisons describe it as
            reporting cadence and running-form metrics but not pace or distance, so it cannot stand
            in for a foot pod on a treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Polar Stride Sensor:</strong> Polar's US store no longer lists it — the
            product address now redirects to Polar's general sensor page — and the UK page we
            checked showed it out of stock. Not available to US buyers from Polar, so excluded.</li>
            <li><strong>Wahoo TICKR X:</strong> no longer on Wahoo's site. Wahoo's current chest
            strap, the TRACKR, replaced the TICKR and TICKR X, and independent reviews note it
            dropped the running-dynamics features. Excluded.</li>
            <li><strong>Milestone Pod:</strong> the brand's web address now hosts an unrelated
            business, and the retailers we checked list the pod as unavailable. Excluded.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            That left Stryd and the COROS POD 2 from the original list. To give readers a real
            choice we added three currently sold alternatives that do the same job in a different
            way: Garmin's HRM 600 chest strap and HRM-Fit clip-on sensor, and the Runn sensor from North
            Pole Engineering, which Zwift lists as its compatible treadmill sensor. Stryd's two-pod Duo,
            which an earlier version of this page carded, is sold direct by Stryd rather than on Amazon,
            so the Garmin HRM-Fit, which is sold on Amazon, took its place.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety check.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's
            recall database for every brand on this page — Stryd, COROS, Garmin and North Pole
            Engineering — and for the excluded Wahoo, Polar, Zwift and Milestone products. We found
            no recall or CPSC warning for any of them. None of these sensors plugs into the mains;
            all are battery powered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bluetooth-vs-ant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Bluetooth vs ANT+: Which Connection You Need</h2>
<p>
            A foot pod is a small radio. It is only useful if it speaks the same language as the
            thing you want it to talk to, and there are two languages in running: Bluetooth Low
            Energy and ANT+.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bluetooth</h3>
          <p>
            Bluetooth is what phones, tablets, Apple TVs, most laptops and every app store app use.
            If you run Zwift on an iPad, an iPhone, an Apple TV or most Windows laptops without
            extra hardware, the pod needs to connect over Bluetooth. The catch is that Bluetooth
            sensors have historically been limited in how many devices can listen at once, so a pod
            already connected to your watch may not also be available to Zwift. Check whether your
            watch and the app can share the pod, or whether the watch can pass the data on.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">ANT+</h3>
          <p>
            ANT+ is the older sports-sensor standard, and its great strength is that any number of
            devices can listen to one sensor at the same time. A Garmin watch and a Zwift computer
            can both receive an ANT+ pod with no conflict. The weakness is that phones and tablets
            generally do not have ANT+ radios. Zwift's support page says that if you are using an
            ANT+ device you will need an ANT+ adapter or a bridge connection, which is exactly why
            the discontinued Garmin Foot Pod, which Zwift lists as ANT+ only, frustrated so many
            iPad users.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What that means for the picks</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Stryd 5.0 and Stryd Duo 5.0</strong> broadcast both. Stryd's store says
            the pod connects over ANT+ and Bluetooth, and its help centre describes separate light
            signals for each. This is the main reason Stryd is the default recommendation: it fits
            almost any combination of watch and app.</li>
            <li><strong>COROS POD 2</strong> pairs with COROS watches over Bluetooth, according to
            COROS's own FAQ. One retailer listing mentions ANT+, but COROS does not, so do not buy
            it expecting ANT+.</li>
            <li><strong>Garmin HRM 600</strong> transmits over both, but the pace and distance
            feature is for compatible Garmin watches; it is not described as a general-purpose
            foot pod for other brands or apps.</li>
            <li><strong>Runn</strong> broadcasts both, and Zwift's page describes it as adding
            Bluetooth speed transmission to compatible treadmills.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift guide</a> goes further into pairing order and the common dropout problems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compatibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Watches and Apps Each Sensor Works With</h2>
<p>
            Compatibility is the real buying decision, and it is where listings are least helpful.
            The table below shows what each maker, or Zwift, states. "Not stated" means we could not
            find the maker or the watch brand confirming it, not that it definitely does not work.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sensor</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Watches</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Zwift</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stryd 5.0 / Duo 5.0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stryd's help centre lists Garmin, COROS, Polar, Suunto and Apple Watch (through the Stryd app)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes — Stryd footpods are on Zwift's list</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">COROS POD 2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">COROS watches only (not the original PACE)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not on Zwift's list</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin HRM 600</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Compatible Garmin watches, for pace and distance</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not as a speed source</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NPE Runn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ANT+ and Bluetooth devices; a review reports Garmin pairs it as a foot pod</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes — Zwift's listed treadmill sensor</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Three practical notes. First, <strong>Apple Watch</strong> is the awkward one. Apple's
            own documentation for the Workout app covers gym equipment through GymKit, but we found
            no Apple documentation of pairing a running foot pod. Stryd supports <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apple-watch/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Apple Watch</a> through its own watch app; its compatibility list was last updated in 2025 and does not
            yet name the newest models, so check before buying. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a> covers the alternatives.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, <strong>COROS owners</strong> cannot run a Stryd and a POD 2 together: COROS's
            FAQ says the watch does not have enough Bluetooth connections for both. Pick one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Third, <strong>Polar watches</strong> pair with Bluetooth stride sensors, according to
            Polar's current user manuals, and Polar no longer sells its own sensor in the US. A
            Stryd is the obvious route for a Polar runner. If you are still choosing the watch
            itself, our roundup of the <a href="/best-running-watches-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best running watches for treadmill running</a> sorts current models by what they do indoors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Calibration: What Each Sensor Asks of You</h2>
<p>
            Every one of these sensors is an estimate, and the way each one is calibrated tells you a
            lot about how it is meant to be used. Before calibrating anything, check the treadmill.
            Pods are often calibrated against a console that reads a few percent fast, which simply
            teaches the pod the same error. Our guide on <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a> explains why consoles drift, and <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration</a> shows the mark-and-stopwatch check.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stryd: designed not to need it</h3>
          <p>
            Stryd's help centre says the pod is accurate for pace and distance out of the box and
            that most users are best served without a calibration factor. Owners on Zwift's forum
            report a mix of experiences: some see their Stryd match a checked treadmill closely,
            others see a small consistent gap, and several point out that the treadmill was the
            inaccurate party. The sensible approach is to check the belt first, then decide whether
            any difference is worth correcting. Zwift's own advice, for all footpods, is to
            calibrate before your first run in Zwift.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">COROS POD 2: automatic</h3>
          <p>
            COROS says the POD 2 calibrates itself automatically by measuring your stride, then
            sends treadmill pace and distance to the watch. COROS's help centre describes the pod as
            the most reliable way to get pace and distance indoors on its watches.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Garmin HRM 600: through the watch</h3>
          <p>
            The strap sends its data to a compatible Garmin watch, which works out pace and
            distance. Garmin's Forerunner manuals also describe a calibrate-and-save step at the end
            of a treadmill run, where you enter the distance from the treadmill. That only helps if the
            treadmill distance is right.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Runn: it reads the belt</h3>
          <p>
            Runn is different in kind: it times marks on the belt rather than estimating your
            stride, so its accuracy depends on the installation, not on you. An independent review
            reports that it ships factory-calibrated and warns that calibrating it to match the
            treadmill console can make it less accurate, not more. That is the point of a belt
            sensor: it can expose a console that reads fast.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Batteries, Charging and Living With a Sensor</h2>
<p>
            All five sensors on this page are rechargeable, which is a change from the coin-cell
            pods of a few years ago. That removes the annual battery swap but adds one more thing to
            charge, and a pod with a flat battery fails silently: your watch falls back to wrist
            estimates and you may not notice until the run is saved.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Stryd 5.0 and Duo 5.0:</strong> Stryd quotes around a month of typical
            running or 20-plus hours of continuous running, and reviewers give the same figure of
            about 20 hours. It charges on a two-sided magnetic keychain charger, and a double tap on
            the pod shows the battery level.</li>
            <li><strong>COROS POD 2:</strong> charges in a portable dock that COROS says holds
            several full charges. COROS and two independent listings give the same running-time
            figure, but another retailer quotes different hours and standby days, so we have left
            the number off the card. Expect a long battery either way.</li>
            <li><strong>Garmin HRM 600:</strong> Garmin rates it at up to two months at an hour a
            day, recharged on a Garmin plug charger.</li>
            <li><strong>Runn:</strong> reviewers describe a rechargeable battery charged over USB.
            North Pole Engineering does not give a runtime on its store page, so neither do we.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Mounting matters as much as charge. A shoe pod should sit straight and tight on the
            laces; COROS's guidance, for example, is that the POD 2 should be as straight as
            possible on shoes with angled lacing. Swap it between shoes deliberately rather than
            leaving it on a pair you no longer run in. The <a href="/treadmill-foot-pod-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">foot pod guide</a> has more on mounting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Read a Foot Pod Listing</h2>
<p>
            Foot pod listings are short, and the important information is usually missing rather
            than wrong. Four checks will save most buyers a return.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. Is it still made?</h3>
          <p>
            Marketplace listings for discontinued pods stay up for years. If a listing names the
            Zwift RunPod, the Garmin Foot Pod, the Milestone Pod or the Polar Stride Sensor, assume
            it is old stock or second-hand. Open the maker's own store and confirm the product is
            listed there today. If the maker has stopped selling it, firmware updates and support
            have usually stopped too.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. Which radio, in which direction?</h3>
          <p>
            "Bluetooth" on a box tells you the pod can talk to something, not that it can talk to
            your watch and your app at the same time. Look for both ANT+ and Bluetooth if you want
            the pod on a watch and in Zwift together, and check whether the listing says the pod
            pairs only with one brand's watches. "Compatible with all major apps" is not a
            compatibility list; a maker's help-centre page naming watch models is.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. Pace, or only running form?</h3>
          <p>
            Some running sensors report cadence, ground contact time and vertical oscillation
            without ever reporting speed. The discontinued Garmin Running Dynamics Pod is the best
            known example, and it is still widely listed. If the listing does not say pace and
            distance, or says them only "with a compatible watch", read the maker's page before
            assuming it will replace your watch's wrist estimate on a treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">4. What does the price include?</h3>
          <p>
            Stryd shows two prices for the same pod: one for the hardware alone and a lower one
            that requires a paid membership commitment. Neither is hidden, but it is easy to compare
            the wrong number with a rival pod. Compare hardware prices with hardware prices, and
            check the current price of any membership yourself before committing, since subscription
            terms change more often than hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            Battery hours are the other figure to treat with care. On the COROS POD 2, the maker
            and two independent listings agree but another retailer gives different numbers, which
            is common. A battery claim matters less on a rechargeable pod than it once did on a coin
            cell; what matters more is whether you will notice when it is flat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pod-or-alternative" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Foot Pod, Belt Sensor or a Connected Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            A foot pod is the right purchase for many treadmill runners, but not for all of them.
            Before you spend $100 to $600, check whether you already have something that does the
            job.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your treadmill broadcasts Bluetooth FTMS</strong> and your app supports it,
            you may not need a sensor at all: the treadmill sends its own speed. Zwift publishes a
            list of <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill brands</a> with confirmed compatible models, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for Zwift</a> roundup covers the machines whose makers name Zwift. Remember that the treadmill sends
            what its console believes, so the speed is only as accurate as the console.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you run on one home treadmill and share it,</strong> the Runn has an
            advantage no pod can match: it measures the belt, so it works for whoever is running,
            with nothing on anyone's shoe. It is a poor choice for gyms or hotels, where you cannot
            leave a sensor and stickers on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you run indoors and outdoors,</strong> a shoe pod moves with you. That is the
            strongest argument for a Stryd: the same sensor serves the treadmill, the hotel gym and
            the road.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you only walk,</strong> skip the pod. Treadmill walking pace is easy to read
            off the console, and the value of a pod is in running pace, where wrist estimates are at
            their weakest. And if heart rate rather than pace is what you want on screen, a strap is
            the better buy; see the <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best heart rate monitors for treadmill training</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, if you are deciding between keeping a basic treadmill and buying a connected
            one, our <a href="/smart-treadmill-vs-regular/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart versus regular treadmill</a> comparison makes the wider case. For most people with a treadmill they like, a pod is
            the cheaper fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill Foot Pod</h2>
          <p>Most foot pod disappointments come from compatibility, not accuracy. These are the four we see most often.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a pod your watch cannot read</h3>
              <p>The COROS POD 2 works only with COROS watches, the HRM 600 gives pace only to compatible Garmin watches, and Apple’s own Workout app documents no foot pod pairing. Check the pod maker’s compatibility list for your exact watch model, not just the brand, before ordering. If you might change watch brands, a pod that speaks both ANT+ and Bluetooth keeps your options open.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ordering a discontinued pod from an old list</h3>
              <p>Many roundups still recommend the Zwift RunPod, the Garmin Foot Pod and the Milestone Pod, all of which have been discontinued. Marketplace listings for them are usually old stock or used units with no support. Zwift will still pair a RunPod, but you are buying hardware nobody will fix or replace. Confirm a product is on its maker’s store today.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring Bluetooth and ANT+</h3>
              <p>An ANT+ only sensor needs an adapter or bridge to reach Zwift on most tablets, phones and laptops, and a Bluetooth sensor already connected to your watch may not be free for Zwift at the same time. Decide where the data needs to go — watch, app or both — and choose a sensor that can reach every destination.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Calibrating to an unchecked console</h3>
              <p>Teaching a pod to agree with a treadmill console that reads a few percent fast makes the pod wrong too, and every run afterwards inherits the error. Measure the belt’s real speed with a mark, a tape and a stopwatch first, then decide whether the pod needs adjusting at all. Stryd says its pod usually does not.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Buy the Stryd 5.0</strong> if you want one pace sensor that works
            with Zwift and with most running watches; it is expensive, but nothing else currently
            sold covers as many devices. COROS owners should buy the <strong class="text-white">COROS POD 2</strong> for $99, and Garmin owners who would
            rather not clip anything to a shoe can use the <strong class="text-white">HRM 600</strong> instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, check the treadmill first — a pod calibrated to a console that reads
            fast just copies the error. Our <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill calibration guide</a> shows the ten-minute belt check.
          </p>
        </div>

