        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best foot pod for treadmill running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most runners in October 2026, the Stryd 5.0, because it broadcasts both ANT+ and Bluetooth, pairs with most running watch brands and is on Zwift's compatible footpod list. COROS watch owners should buy the COROS POD 2 instead, which costs far less but works only with COROS watches. Garmin owners can use the HRM 600 chest strap for treadmill pace.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which foot pods work with Zwift?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Zwift's compatible hardware page lists the Zwift RunPod, Stryd footpods, the Polar Stride Sensor and the Garmin Foot Pod (ANT+ only), plus the Runn as a treadmill sensor. Of those, the RunPod and Garmin pod are discontinued and Polar no longer sells its sensor in the US, so Stryd and Runn are the practical new purchases. Zwift recommends calibrating a footpod before use.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Zwift RunPod still sold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Zwift's own RunPod FAQ says the RunPod has been discontinued and is no longer available to purchase. It is still on Zwift's compatibility list, so a second-hand unit will pair, though at least one owner on Zwift's forum called it inaccurate. For a new purchase, a Stryd footpod or the Runn treadmill sensor are the options Zwift lists that are still sold.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Garmin Running Dynamics Pod give pace on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not as a foot pod replacement. Garmin forum replies and independent comparisons describe it as reporting cadence and running-form metrics such as ground contact time and vertical oscillation, not pace or distance. It also appears to be discontinued: retailers list it that way and its US product page no longer loads. Garmin now points runners to the HRM 600 for treadmill pace.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need to calibrate a foot pod on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the pod. Stryd says its pod is accurate out of the box and most users need no calibration factor, and COROS says the POD 2 calibrates itself. Zwift still recommends calibrating any footpod before use. Whatever you do, check the treadmill's real belt speed first, because calibrating to a console that reads fast copies its error.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a foot pod with an Apple Watch?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not through any method Apple documents for its own Workout app, as far as we could find. Stryd supports Apple Watch through the Stryd watch app, though its published compatibility list predates the newest models. The COROS POD 2 and Garmin HRM 600 do not work with Apple Watch for pace. On GymKit-equipped gym treadmills, the treadmill can send its data to the watch instead.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

